EXT. ORCHARD BEHIND A CLOSED CANNERY - LATE SUMMER AFTERNOON

Pears hang heavy on the branches. Wasps drone over fallen fruit. Beyond the trees, a corrugated roof rusts under a pale sky.

NELL, 15, sits with her back against a trunk, knees drawn up. She has a transistor radio in her lap, tuned to nothing. A paper sack of pears lies beside her. Dust on her shoes. A bruise yellowing on one shin.

She bites a pear. Chews. Does not taste it.

A twig snaps. She looks up. A boy has stepped from between the trees, hands in his pockets. THEO, 16, tall and awkward, wearing his older brother's jacket. He stops ten paces away.

He studies her. She studies the pear.

THEO
You're on the Dunnings' side.

NELL
Is there a side?

He shrugs. Scuffs a pear into the grass with his toe. She watches the movement and then the dirt.

THEO
They said you left.

NELL
They said a lot.

He nods slowly. She offers him a pear without looking up. He crosses, takes it, sits a careful distance away. They eat in silence. A wasp circles his hand. He lets it.

THEO
My brother's gone. Since June.

She says nothing. Turns the dial on the radio. Static swells, then a thin fragment of a song. She holds still, listening. He leans in a little. The song fades.

THEO (CONT'D)
That was a good one.

NELL
It always is. Then it goes.

He half-smiles. She turns the dial again. Nothing. She sets the radio on the grass between them.

A screen door bangs somewhere beyond the trees. A woman's voice calls a name that is not hers. Nell's shoulders rise. Theo watches them rise.

THEO (CONT'D)
That's for you?

NELL
That's for a girl I was.

He nods as if this makes sense. Stands. Offers his hand. She looks at it for a long moment. Then takes it. He pulls her up. They let go at the same time.

At the edge of the orchard, an older figure steps out: MRS. DUNNING, 50s, apron, arms folded. She stops when she sees them both. Her face softens. Hardens. Softens again.

Nell straightens. Theo steps half a pace in front of her. Mrs. Dunning looks at his jacket, at the radio on the grass, at the pear core in Nell's hand.

She turns and walks back toward the house. Leaves the door open behind her.

Nell picks up the radio. Theo picks up the sack of pears. They follow, ten paces apart, then five.
